</p/><p><strong>Summary: </strong>
In preparation for my #Stonetop #ttprg session, I wrote several questions to walk through a flashback sequence.  I felt it was a rewarding experience keeping the story in the present, yet giving access to the past actions.
</p>
        <p>In our Stonetop campaign, one of the character’s tall-tales involved sneaking to
the ruined tower with her friend, finding some magic tablets, feeling a sense
that they had become barren, and then burying those tablets in the outer berm of
the ruined tower.</p>
<p>During the 2nd session, the characters recovered the tablets and as we wrapped
up indicated that they were going to delve further into the ruined tower.  Which
had me wondering, where and how did the character discover the tablets?  Also,
how do I reveal that these tablets may serve an important purpose?</p>
<p>Further, everyone wants the characters to get back to Stonetop, such that the
players thought that the 3rd session should end with their return.  And the 4th
session should really be about spring in the village.</p>
<p>I envision (establish?) that the original housing for the tablets would be
deeper underground.  Yet, I don’t want such a deep delve so early in the
campaign.</p>
<p>With that in mind, I set about writing up questions around the character’s
childhood visit to the ruined tower.</p>
<h1 id="questions-from-session-two">Questions from Session Two</h1>
<p>First, let’s revisit the second session question I asked of the player
character:</p>

<blockquote  class="h-cite">

How will you remember the location of the runic tablets you buried outside the
Ruined Tower amidst the overgrown outer berm?

</blockquote>

<p>The question I asked guided the player on where I assumed they would’ve stashed
the tablets.  Instead of me asking the player “where did you stash this thing”
and requiring them to consider the entire location, which is less relevant to
play, I opted to create a more murky situation; namely having the character
share how they’d remember.</p>
<p>This also made finding the tablet a straight forward mechanical move.</p>
<p>In session, we settled on the <em>Seek Insight</em> move for finding the tablets some 20
years later.  Were I to do it again, I think I would have chosen a <em>Defy Danger</em>
move.</p>
<h1 id="questions-for-session-three">Questions for Session Three</h1>
<p>With the <span><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Player_character">Player Characters</a></span> (<abbr title="Player Characters">PCs</abbr> <small><a class="ref" rel="tag opener" aria-label="Other site-wide references of “Player Characters”" title="Other site-wide references of “Player Characters”" href="https://takeonrules.com/site-map/glossary/#abbr-dfn-GLOSSARY-PC">&#128214;</a></small>)
 intending to visit the ruined tower, and we’ve established that in
the player character’s past they found the tables, I started wondering how I’d
let their character’s background memories influence the play at the table.</p>
<p>So I built out a series of directed and constraining questions that would show
and share with the present characters the past actions of that character.</p>

<blockquote  class="h-cite">

Without torch or lantern, that score or so years ago, you chose to ascend the
second floor…and then the third…why did you almost turn back after the second
floor?

</blockquote>

<p>This question set the state for sneaking into the tower, giving space for
reconsideration.</p>

<blockquote  class="h-cite">

That night on the third floor, sleeping beside Mair what noise told you there
were wicked men at the ground floor?

</blockquote>

<p>This highlighted the danger of the ruins, and that others visit the location.</p>

<blockquote  class="h-cite">

Tell me what flashed through your mind when the screams of their deaths pierced
your dreams?

</blockquote>

<p>And now to pivot, highlighting the danger.</p>

<blockquote  class="h-cite">

As you descended in the morning what word or two comes to mind of the shape you
saw receding into the lower levels…satisfied with fulfilling its murderous duty?

</blockquote>

<p>And give space for the Tesatu’s player to paint a bit of a picture regarding a
possible future threat.</p>

<blockquote  class="h-cite">

What do you must remember of the wicked looking pendant on one of the dead men
at the ground floor?

</blockquote>

<p>A little bit of color, drawing attention to a possible artifact.  But
discouraging picking it up (out of superstition).</p>

<blockquote  class="h-cite">

What was it about the runic tablets that caught your attention?

</blockquote>

<p>This was the thing that struck Tesatu barren.  It was the thing she buried in
the berm.  Why did she pick it up?</p>

<blockquote  class="h-cite">

When did the dreamlike memories of this scene of carnage finally abate?

</blockquote>

<p>And now to ask about the lingering weight of this encounter.</p>

<blockquote  class="h-cite">

How do you feel as you recount these memories to your friends?

</blockquote>

<p>Then to draw the characters back into the present and give knowledge to all of
the player characters.  In hindsight, I forced the character to choose, which
violates the principle of consent.  I should’ve asked if they were sharing this.
But the player received it with grace, and we gained some deeper insight into
the pathos of Tesatu.</p>
<h1 id="conclusion">Conclusion</h1>
<p>These directed questions helped my players relieve a past moment, without
dwelling on it, and instead filling them with vignettes.  And from that, further
soft and hard moves naturally flowed.</p>

